活動内容:
We are the only Sports Chanbara club at Waseda University. Sports Chanbara, or “Spo-chan” for short, is a new martial art that originated in Japan about 50 years ago. Using soft, inflatable swords, members engage in one-on-one battles on a court, with the first person to strike their opponent’s body declared the winner. Because it is a minor sport, anyone can compete in national-level tournaments. We also interact with Chanbara enthusiasts nationwide, extending beyond our club’s boundaries. ///
///////
////Most of our members started Sports Chanbara in college, and both our men’s and women’s teams achieve numerous accomplishments every year, including national championship titles. Since we practice twice a week after classes, we have a diverse membership that includes students from both the humanities and sciences. Whether you’re looking to try something new at university or want to get more exercise, everyone is welcome! Please feel free to contact us anytime!

The Waseda University Geography Research Society is a historic inter-university geography club celebrating its 74th year this year. /
///
//Geography tends to have a reputation for being a dry, academic subject, but within our club, we include everything from cities, nature, commerce, and transportation—basically anything related to our daily lives—under the umbrella of “geography,” so there’s no need to worry! /
///
//From members who are passionate about geography to those who are just curious, we have a diverse group of members, so anyone can enjoy being part of our club. /
///
//Our activities consist of field trips held at least once a month, one or two annual retreats, and occasional club meetings and game events. /
///
//Field trips are day trips where we explore towns in the Tokyo area. Guided by a navigator (the trip organizer), we discover the charm of these towns from a geographical perspective. /
///
//Retreats involve spending several days conducting field trips in specific regions across Japan. Recent destinations have included San’in, Izu, and Aso… /
///
//We also occasionally hold club meetings as a forum for members to present their research without limiting the themes. /
///
//And recently, a popular activity has been team-based games where we visit various spots around Tokyo (such as Real Momotaro, Geography Quest, Bus Route Connection Journey, and mystery-solving games)! These aren’t just for fun—they’re opportunities to actively combine knowledge and demonstrate our spatial geographical understanding! /
///
//In addition, we participate in the Waseda Festival, create our club magazine for the event, and have thriving “sub-circles” within the club where members can delve deeply into specific interests. /
///
///
///
//Why not join us and view the everyday scenery from a slightly more sophisticated perspective?

The Archaeology Research Society is recruiting members year-round! Since we’re the “Archaeology Research Society,” anyone interested in archaeology is, of course, very welcome, but we also warmly welcome those interested in history, religious studies, linguistics, and more! We have plenty of upperclassmen who share those interests! Also, our club room is twice as large and comfortable as a typical club’s, so members often gather there during free periods, making it easy to bond with one another! Plus!!New students get their first semester’s membership fee waived!! Our main activities consist of study sessions held once or twice a week! We also organize day trips during orientation week, take overnight trips to visit historical sites across the country in the spring and summer, and volunteer at local archaeological events! If you’re even slightly interested, please feel free to drop by Room E501 in the Student Union Building!Most of our members are from Waseda’s School of Literature, but we also have students from other schools, as well as students from other universities and international students—so everyone is more than welcome! We’re active on both X (@waseda_koukoken) and Instagram (waseda_koukoken), and you can also contact us via Gmail ([email protected]). Waseda students, why not spend a fun college life with the Archaeology Research Club? We look forward to seeing you!/
///
///
///
// Email us here: [email protected]

We’re a group of people who enjoy relaxing and gazing at the starry sky. At our weekly meetings, we use the club’s telescopes to observe the sky from the rooftop of the Student Union Building, visit planetariums, go to observatories, and engage in various other activities related to stars and the universe. We also build a large planetarium for the Waseda Festival. /
///
// We also hold many overnight trips dedicated to stargazing. In places far from the city center, like Nagano and Izu Oshima, you can see a sky full of stars! We warmly welcome everyone—whether you’re an expert on the stars or just want to see a beautiful night sky. It’s a laid-back club, so feel free to join at your own pace. Let’s observe the Milky Way, shooting stars, and countless constellations together!

We are a club that practices "shorthand," a technique for transcribing spoken text using a special set of characters. /
///
///
///
//Practices are held during the 5th period on weekdays, and you can join at any time that suits your schedule. Senior members provide instruction tailored to each person’s progress, so even if you can’t attend many practices, you can learn shorthand at your own pace. In addition to shorthand practice, we hold many events such as seasonal camps, Christmas parties, and outings. Participation in both practices and events is completely optional. Therefore, it is entirely possible to balance this with your studies and other activities. /
///
///
///
//Whether you’re looking to develop a new skill, try something different, or find a place to belong at university, everyone is welcome. If you’re even slightly interested, please come check us out! Let’s enjoy shorthand together!!

The Waseda University Animation Research Society (WAFL) is an officially recognized intercollegiate club at Waseda University and the only independent production club on campus where students can “create” animations. /
///
//We are divided into various departments—including animation, coloring, art, sound, filming, editing, and 3D—to produce our works. /
///
///
///
//You might be thinking, “I’ve never made an anime before—will I be okay?” But of course you will! Almost all of our members started from scratch, so beginners are more than welcome./
///
///
///
//Our main activity is producing animations for joint screenings held in the summer and winter with independent production clubs from other universities. We also participate in events like the Waseda Festival. Additionally, we’re very active, going on summer and winter retreats and going to the movies together as a club. /
///
///
///
//If you have even the slightest interest in animation production, you’re more than welcome—even as a beginner! We’re also happy to have members from other universities join us, and we welcome new members at any time, so please feel free to stop by!/
///
///
///
//Check out our works, behind-the-scenes videos, and recruitment information on Twitter and YouTube!

Among the many band clubs, this club specializes in performing so-called "black music," such as soul, funk, and R&B. Centered around summer and winter recitals, we organize about 10 live performances a year and practice diligently every day in preparation. The Naleo owns a variety of equipment, and when we hold live performances in the Student Union Building, we handle everything from setup to operation ourselves. As a result, our members have a deep understanding of sound engineering and event planning.//
///
///We operate on a fixed band system, where members stay in the same band for three years to refine and pursue their own musical style. Additionally, as an intercollegiate club, we transcend university boundaries, allowing members to meet friends purely through music.//
///
///In addition to band practice, we hold jam sessions in the club room whenever people gather. We welcome non-members, so you can make music and connect with people from other universities and the general public.//
///
///In 2016, we held a party at Blue Note Tokyo to commemorate our 70th anniversary, where current members performed live. Because of our long history, we have close ties with alumni, allowing you to experience things up close that ordinary students wouldn’t normally get to experience.

We are the a cappella club Choco Crunch!/
//
/Choco Crunch is an intercollegiate club founded in 2000, with members from various universities. We also have many members from science and engineering fields and students in their second year or higher, so you can interact with a diverse group of people. Many members join with no prior musical experience, and the appeal is that anyone can enjoy it. /
//
/Our motto is “Let’s have fun and improve together”!/
//
/In addition to general rehearsals held about twice a month, we also hold activities for each a cappella band. In these band activities, we perform a wide variety of songs across all genres. Members can enjoy a cappella in ways that suit their individual goals, whether it’s aiming to perform at our biannual club concerts, enjoying internal performances, or competing in external competitions. Choco Crunch bands have a track record of success, including winning division championships at national competitions./
//
/We also have a rich lineup of non-a cappella events, including two annual training camps, Halloween and Christmas parties, field trips, and bowling outings. Through these events, you’re sure to make friends with everyone, regardless of year, class, age, or gender!

During our weekly group sessions—held every Tuesday from 1:00 PM to 8:00 PM and every Friday from 1:00 PM to 8:00 PM—members freely engage in various activities, ranging from creating art using a variety of media such as plaster casting, oil painting, watercolor, and digital art, to casual conversation and working on lecture assignments. After the sessions, we often have dinner together, hold small get-togethers over drinks, or just relax in the club room. /
///
///
///
//We also have plenty of opportunities to showcase our work. We hold exhibitions about once a month, organizing solo shows or participating in joint exhibitions with other universities. This year, we held an exhibition at a university-run gallery and also collaborated on numerous projects and exhibitions with other Waseda University clubs and art clubs from other universities. On days off and during long breaks, we go on museum tours, attend events, and hold retreats to strengthen the bonds among members./
///
//We highly recommend this club to anyone looking for a well-balanced group that enriches both your artistic and campus life!

ALOHA!! We are the Charter School Educational Support ~Hawaii Edition~ (Hawaii PJ), an officially recognized educational support volunteer club at Waseda University that originated from the Waseda University Ikuo Hirayama Memorial Volunteer Center (WAVOC).Guided by our philosophy of “Learning by Teaching,” we visit a charter school in Volcano Village on the Big Island of Hawaii, USA, every summer and spring break. During a two-week homestay, we teach local children Japanese language and culture classes and engage in activities to deepen our bonds with them.
/
/
/
/* Love education and children!!
/
/* Want to experience American and Hawaiian culture
/
/* Want to step into an overseas educational setting
/
/* Love Japanese culture and want to share the best of Japan with the world
/
/* Interested in international exchange
/
/* I want to actively speak English!
/
/If this sounds like you, why not join us as an educational support volunteer at elementary and middle schools in Hawaii and spend a summer vacation unlike any other? We, too, learn a great deal from the honest and hardworking children there. Additionally, as part of our club’s OHANA (family), we hold pre-trip meetings and study sessions on Hawaiian and Japanese culture before departure, and we also participate in events such as the WAVOC-sponsored presentation contest.
